  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1204 PM MDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nUNSEASONABLY WARM THROUGH FRIDAY: \n\nHigh pressure continues to drive the forecast through the end \nof the work week leading to stagnant conditons across eastern \nUtah and western Colorado. This means that afternoon \ntemperatures will continue to run 10-15 degrees above normal \n(near-record values) and isolated terrain-based showers and \nstorms are possible during peak heating hours. \n\nWET WEEKEND AHEAD: \n\nOur focus remains on the incoming pattern change this weekend. \nAt the same time as low pressure builds along the West Coast, \nTropical Storm Rachel is expected to approach the coast of \nnorthern Baja California and southern California. Locally, this \nwill lead to a sizable surge of tropical moisture across \nportions of the Desert Southwest, including eastern Utah and \nwestern Colorado. As PWATs build towards 250-275% of normal, \novercast skies will overtake the region, followed by widespread \nrainfall favoring locations south of I-70 Saturday evening into \nSunday. \n\nThe current through is that a bulk of this precipitation will \nfall in the form of rain at all elevations, with light to \nmoderate stratiform rain rates. Embedded thunderstorms are \npossible from time to time. Most storms will be of the garden \nvariety, but if stronger storms develop, flooding concerns will \nincrease drastically. We'll have a clearer picture once more hi-\nres guidance encompasses this timeframe over the next 24 hours.\nIf stronger thunderstorms develop, heavy rain rates could lead \nto localized flash flooding and quick rises along streams and \nrivers.\n\nCOOLER, CONTINUED WET WEATHER NEXT WEEK: \n\nForecast confidence is very low beyond the weekend, but we \ngenerally expect continued wet weather and a gradual transition \ntowards cooler temperatures. As soon as the main push of remnant\nmoisture from Rachel exits to the northeast, another tropical \nsystem (Simon) enters the picture. Over the last 24 hours, \ninland moisture advection from Simon has trended to the east, \naway from our local forecast area. However, this track will be \nlargely dependent on the evolution of the aformentioned low \npressure system to our west. \n\nIf the current forecast holds, low pressure will move across \nthe Great Basin towards the local area by midweek. A cold front \nassociated with this system will cause a notable drop in \ntemperatures and snow levels, leading to increasing chances for \nsnow, especially above 10,000 feet. This is a very fluid \nforecast and we expect the details to change between now and \nnext week. For now, be aware that rapidly changing weather \nconditions are possible next week.",
